# No Unicode in Code

## Rule
**NEVER use Unicode characters (including emojis, special symbols, checkmarks, etc.) in any code files.**

## Scope
This rule applies to:
- Python scripts
- Configuration files
- Test files
- Any executable code
- Print statements
- Comments
- String literals

## Allowed
- Standard ASCII characters only (letters, numbers, basic punctuation)
- Standard ASCII symbols: `!@#$%^&*()_+-=[]{}|;:'",.<>?/~`

## Prohibited
- Unicode checkmarks: ✓ ✗ ✘
- Unicode arrows: → ← ↑ ↓
- Emojis: 😀 ✅ ❌ ⚠️ 📋 etc.
- Special Unicode symbols: • ▪ ▫ etc.
- Any non-ASCII characters

## Examples

### ❌ BAD
```python
print("✓ PASS: Test passed")
print("✗ FAIL: Test failed")
print("⚠️ Warning: Issue found")
status = "✅ Complete"
```

### ✅ GOOD
```python
print("[PASS] Test passed")
print("[FAIL] Test failed")
print("[WARNING] Issue found")
status = "[COMPLETE]"
```

## Rationale
Unicode characters can cause encoding errors in Windows environments, especially with cp1252 encoding. Using ASCII-only characters ensures code runs reliably across all platforms and environments.

## Enforcement
When writing or modifying code:
1. Replace Unicode checkmarks with `[PASS]` / `[FAIL]` / `[OK]` / `[ERROR]`
2. Replace emojis with descriptive text in brackets
3. Use ASCII-only symbols for visual indicators
4. Test that code runs without UnicodeEncodeError exceptions




















